194	Semantic error detection &
195	  Four negative type-checker tests in \code{D3/semantic/}; each rejected
196	  with a precise, line-numbered diagnostic. \\
197	Runtime error handling &
198	  Two runtime-error tests in \code{D3/semantic/}; each aborts with an
199	  informative message and exits \code{1}. \\
200	D1 consistency &
201	  Static scoping, call-by-value, strong typing, no implicit coercion, and
202	  reaction-balance verification are all exercised by the three programs. \\
203	\bottomrule
204	\end{tabularx}
205	\end{table}
206	
207	% =====================================================================
208	\section{End-to-End Execution of Three Valid Programs}
209	\sectionrule
210	
211	All three programs are executed with \code{D2/ans --run <file>}.
212	The type checker runs first; if it passes, the interpreter executes the program.
213	
214	% ----- Program 1 -----
215	\subsection{Program 1 --- Molar Mass Classifier (\file{D3/programs/molar\_mass\_demo.ans})}
216	
217	\textbf{Features exercised:} \code{element} and \code{compound} declarations, typed
218	function parameter (\code{compound c}), \code{for}-in loop over a Formula
219	(§4.4.1), member access (\code{c.formula}, \code{element.atomic\_mass}),
220	formula indexing (\code{formula[element]}), augmented assignment (\code{mass +=}),
221	\code{if}/\code{elif}/\code{else} with Boolean comparison, list literal and
222	\code{for}-in over a List.
223	
224	\begin{lstlisting}[style=ans]
225	element H { atomic_mass = 1.008,  valence = 1, symbol = "H" };
226	element O { atomic_mass = 15.999, valence = 2, symbol = "O" };
227	element C { atomic_mass = 12.011, valence = 4, symbol = "C" };
228	element N { atomic_mass = 14.007, valence = 3, symbol = "N" };
229	
230	compound water   { formula = H2O,  name = "Water"          };
231	compound methane { formula = CH4,  name = "Methane"        };
232	compound co2     { formula = CO2,  name = "Carbon dioxide" };
233	compound ammonia { formula = NH3,  name = "Ammonia"        };
234	
235	function molar_mass(compound c) {
236	    let formula = c.formula;
237	    let mass = 0.0;
238	    for (let element in formula) {
239	        mass += element.atomic_mass * formula[element];
240	    }
241	    return mass;
242	};
243	
244	function classify_mass(compound c) {
245	    let mass = molar_mass(c);
246	    if (mass < 20.0) {
247	        print "Light molecule.";
248	    }
249	    elif (mass < 50.0) {
250	        print "Medium molecule.";
251	    }
252	    else {
253	        print "Heavy molecule.";
254	    }
255	};
256	
257	let compounds = [water, methane, co2, ammonia];
258	for (let c in compounds) {
259	    classify_mass(c);
260	};
261	\end{lstlisting}
262	
263	\textbf{Trace.} The \code{for}-in over \code{compounds} (a \code{List<Compound>})
264	calls \code{classify\_mass} four times. Inside each call, \code{molar\_mass}
265	iterates the compound's formula in left-to-right first-appearance order,
266	accumulating $\sum (\textit{atomic\_mass} \times \textit{count})$:
267	
268	\begin{table}[h]
269	\centering
270	\rowcolors{2}{anscodebg}{white}
271	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{} L{2.0cm} L{1.8cm} L{4.8cm} L{1.6cm} L{2.2cm} @{}}
272	\toprule
273	\rowcolor{ansblue}
274	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Compound}} &
275	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Formula}} &
276	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Calculation}} &
277	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Mass (g/mol)}} &
278	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Class}} \\
279	\midrule
280	\code{water}   & H\textsubscript{2}O   & $2{\times}1.008 + 1{\times}15.999$ & 18.015 & Light ($<20$) \\
281	\code{methane} & CH\textsubscript{4}   & $1{\times}12.011 + 4{\times}1.008$ & 16.043 & Light ($<20$) \\
282	\code{co2}     & CO\textsubscript{2}   & $1{\times}12.011 + 2{\times}15.999$ & 44.009 & Medium ($<50$) \\
283	\code{ammonia} & NH\textsubscript{3}   & $1{\times}14.007 + 3{\times}1.008$ & 17.031 & Light ($<20$) \\
284	\bottomrule
285	\end{tabularx}
286	\end{table}
287	
288	\textbf{Expected and actual output:}
289	\begin{lstlisting}[style=terminal]
290	$ D2/ans --run D3/programs/molar_mass_demo.ans
291	Light molecule.
292	Light molecule.
293	Medium molecule.
294	Light molecule.
295	\end{lstlisting}
296	Result: \statusok, exit \code{0}.
297	
298	\newpage
299	
300	% ----- Program 2 -----
301	\subsection{Program 2 --- Combustion Reaction Analysis (\file{D3/programs/combustion\_analysis.ans})}
302	
303	\textbf{Features exercised:} \code{reaction} declaration with \code{check = balanced}
304	and exothermic energy quantity, typed parameters \code{reaction r} and
305	\code{compound c}, \code{reaction.reactants}/\code{reaction.products} member access,
306	ReactionSide indexing (\code{reactants[c]}), built-in \code{mol()}, division,
307	multiplication, and \code{if}/\code{else}.
308	
309	\begin{lstlisting}[style=ans,firstnumber=15]
310	reaction combustion {
311	    reactants = CH4 + 2*O2,
312	    products  = CO2 + 2*H2O,
313	    check     = balanced,
314	    energy    = exothermic 890.3 kJ/mol
315	};
316	
317	function compute_yield(reaction r, compound c) {
318	    let products  = r.products;
319	    let reactants = r.reactants;
320	    let reactant_mol = 1.0 / mol(reactants[c]);
321	    let product_mol  = reactant_mol * mol(products[c]);
322	    return product_mol;
323	};
324	
325	function print_reaction_summary(reaction r, compound fuel, compound product) {
326	    let yield_val = compute_yield(r, fuel);
327	    let mass      = molar_mass(product);
328	    if (yield_val > 0.0) {
329	        print "Reaction produces output.";
330	    }
331	    else {
332	        print "No product formed.";
333	    }
334	};
335	
336	print_reaction_summary(combustion, methane, co2);
337	\end{lstlisting}
338	
339	\textbf{Trace of \code{compute\_yield(combustion, methane)}.}
340	\code{reactants[methane]} looks up the stoichiometric coefficient of
341	\code{methane} in \code{CH4 + 2*O2}: coefficient = \textbf{1.0}.
342	\code{mol(1.0)} returns \textbf{1.0} (strips unit; identity for plain number).
343	\code{reactant\_mol} = $1.0 / 1.0$ = \textbf{1.0}.
344	\code{products[methane]} looks up \code{methane} (formula CH\textsubscript{4}) in
345	\code{CO2 + 2*H2O}: not found, coefficient = \textbf{0.0}.
346	\code{product\_mol} = $1.0 \times 0.0$ = \textbf{0.0}.
347	\code{yield\_val} = \textbf{0.0}; the condition \code{0.0 > 0.0} is false.
348	
349	This is chemically correct: methane is a reactant, not a product of its own
350	combustion, so no methane is produced.
351	
352	\textbf{Expected and actual output:}
353	\begin{lstlisting}[style=terminal]
354	$ D2/ans --run D3/programs/combustion_analysis.ans
355	No product formed.
356	\end{lstlisting}
357	Result: \statusok, exit \code{0}. The reaction balance check (\code{check = balanced})
358	was also verified at interpretation time: both sides produce
359	$\{C{:}1,\ H{:}4,\ O{:}4\}$.
360	
361	% ----- Program 3 -----
362	\subsection{Program 3 --- Multi-Reaction Survey (\file{D3/programs/multi\_reaction\_survey.ans})}
363	
364	\textbf{Features exercised:} Multiple \code{reaction} declarations using both
365	formula-literal and identifier species, \code{for}-in over a ReactionSide
366	(§4.4.1 extended rule), \code{for}-in over a \code{List<Compound>}, augmented
367	assignment to a top-level \code{let} variable across loop iterations,
368	\code{character} literal, and \code{string} literal.
369	
370	\begin{lstlisting}[style=ans,firstnumber=43]
371	let heavy_count = 0;
372	let light_count = 0;
373	let compounds = [methane, oxygen, co2, water, ammonia, nitrogen, hydrogen];
374	
375	for (let c in compounds) {
376	    let mass = molar_mass(c);
377	    if (mass >= 30.0) {
378	        heavy_count += 1;
379	    }
380	    else {
381	        light_count += 1;
382	    }
383	};
384	
385	let sym_h = 'H';
386	let label = "Survey complete";
387	
388	total_reactant_mass(combustion);
389	total_reactant_mass(haber_bosch);
390	\end{lstlisting}
391	
392	\textbf{Trace.} The loop classifies seven compounds:
393	
394	\begin{table}[h]
395	\centering
396	\rowcolors{2}{anscodebg}{white}
397	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{} L{2.0cm} L{1.8cm} L{1.6cm} L{2.0cm} @{}}
398	\toprule
399	\rowcolor{ansblue}
400	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Compound}} &
401	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Formula}} &
402	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Mass}} &
403	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Bucket}} \\
404	\midrule
405	\code{methane}  & CH\textsubscript{4}   & 16.043 & light \\
406	\code{oxygen}   & O\textsubscript{2}    & 31.998 & heavy \\
407	\code{co2}      & CO\textsubscript{2}   & 44.009 & heavy \\
408	\code{water}    & H\textsubscript{2}O   & 18.015 & light \\
409	\code{ammonia}  & NH\textsubscript{3}   & 17.031 & light \\
410	\code{nitrogen} & N\textsubscript{2}    & 28.014 & light \\
411	\code{hydrogen} & H\textsubscript{2}    &  2.016 & light \\
412	\bottomrule
413	\end{tabularx}
414	\end{table}
415	
416	Final state: \code{heavy\_count} = 2, \code{light\_count} = 5.
417	\code{total\_reactant\_mass(combustion)} and
418	\code{total\_reactant\_mass(haber\_bosch)} both execute --- the former uses
419	\code{for}-in over a \code{ReactionSide} --- and return \textbf{48.041} and
420	\textbf{30.030} respectively. Neither is a \code{print} statement, so no output
421	is produced. The program is designed to test that the interpreter executes
422	correctly and silently, demonstrating scoping and loop-counter persistence.
423	
424	\textbf{Expected and actual output:}
425	\begin{lstlisting}[style=terminal]
426	$ D2/ans --run D3/programs/multi_reaction_survey.ans
427	(no output)
428	\end{lstlisting}
429	Result: \statusok, exit \code{0}.
430	
431	% =====================================================================
432	\newpage
433	\section{Type-Checker Semantic Error Detection}
434	\sectionrule
435	
436	The following four programs in \code{D3/semantic/} are syntactically valid (the
437	D3 P1 parser accepts them) but semantically invalid. Each is rejected by
438	\code{D2/ans --check} with a line-numbered, actionable diagnostic. This demonstrates
439	that the type checker implements the strong-typing, formula-validity, and reaction-balance
440	requirements declared in D1 §4.5 and §4.4.
441	
442	\begin{table}[h]
443	\centering
444	\rowcolors{2}{anscodebg}{white}
445	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{} L{4.2cm} Y Y L{1.9cm} @{}}
446	\toprule
447	\rowcolor{ansblue}
448	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{File}} &
449	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Defect}} &
450	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Diagnostic}} &
451	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Result}} \\
452	\midrule
453	\file{sem1\_unbalanced\_reaction.ans} &
454	Reaction \code{bad\_water}: reactants $\{H{:}2, O{:}2\}$, products $\{H{:}2, O{:}1\}$; oxygen count differs. &
455	Reports the line of the \code{reaction} declaration, names the reaction, and states the cause. &
456	\statuserr \\
457	\file{sem2\_undeclared\_element.ans} &
458	Formula \code{NH3} references element \code{N}, which is never declared. &
459	Names the formula, the undeclared element symbol, and the line of the \code{compound} declaration. &
460	\statuserr \\
461	\file{sem3\_missing\_field.ans} &
462	\code{compound water} is declared without the required \code{name} field. &
463	Names the compound and the missing field at the declaration line. &
464	\statuserr \\
465	\file{sem4\_wrong\_arg\_count.ans} &
466	\code{molar\_mass()} called with 0 arguments; function expects 1. &
467	Names the function, the expected count, and the actual count at the call site. &
468	\statuserr \\
469	\bottomrule
470	\end{tabularx}
471	\end{table}
472	
473	\textbf{Actual checker output.}
474	\begin{lstlisting}[style=terminal]
475	=== D3/semantic/sem1_unbalanced_reaction.ans ===
476	$ D2/ans --check D3/semantic/sem1_unbalanced_reaction.ans
477	D3/semantic/sem1_unbalanced_reaction.ans:9:0: error: reaction 'bad_water' is not balanced (atom counts differ)
478	
479	=== D3/semantic/sem2_undeclared_element.ans ===
480	$ D2/ans --check D3/semantic/sem2_undeclared_element.ans
481	D3/semantic/sem2_undeclared_element.ans:7:0: error: formula 'NH3' uses undeclared element 'N'
482	
483	=== D3/semantic/sem3_missing_field.ans ===
484	$ D2/ans --check D3/semantic/sem3_missing_field.ans
485	D3/semantic/sem3_missing_field.ans:6:0: error: compound 'water' missing name
486	
487	=== D3/semantic/sem4_wrong_arg_count.ans ===
488	$ D2/ans --check D3/semantic/sem4_wrong_arg_count.ans
489	D3/semantic/sem4_wrong_arg_count.ans:18:0: error: function 'molar_mass' expects 1 argument(s), got 0
490	\end{lstlisting}
491	
492	% =====================================================================
493	\section{Runtime Error Handling}
494	\sectionrule
495	
496	Two programs in \code{D3/semantic/} exercise the three mandatory runtime error
497	conditions from D2 P2. Each program passes the type checker (no semantic errors)
498	but produces a fatal runtime error that aborts execution cleanly with exit code
499	\code{1} and an informative diagnostic.
500	
501	\begin{table}[h]
502	\centering
503	\rowcolors{2}{anscodebg}{white}
504	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{} L{3.6cm} Y Y L{1.9cm} @{}}
505	\toprule
506	\rowcolor{ansblue}
507	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{File}} &
508	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Defect}} &
509	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Diagnostic}} &
510	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Result}} \\
511	\midrule
512	\file{rt1\_division\_by\_zero.ans} &
513	\code{42.0 / denom} where \code{denom = 0.0}. Detected at run time when the
514	\code{/} operator is applied. &
515	Reports file, line, and message \code{division by zero}. &
516	\statuserr, exit \code{1} \\
517	\file{rt2\_index\_out\_of\_range.ans} &
518	\code{xs[5]} where \code{xs} has length 2. Detected at run time during index
519	evaluation. &
520	Reports file, line, the actual index, and the valid range \code{[0..1]}. &
521	\statuserr, exit \code{1} \\
522	\bottomrule
523	\end{tabularx}
524	\end{table}
525	
526	\textbf{Actual runtime output.}
527	\begin{lstlisting}[style=terminal]
528	=== D3/semantic/rt1_division_by_zero.ans ===
529	$ D2/ans --run D3/semantic/rt1_division_by_zero.ans
530	D3/semantic/rt1_division_by_zero.ans:6: runtime error: division by zero
531	
532	=== D3/semantic/rt2_index_out_of_range.ans ===
533	$ D2/ans --run D3/semantic/rt2_index_out_of_range.ans
534	D3/semantic/rt2_index_out_of_range.ans:12: runtime error: list index 5 out of range [0..1]
535	\end{lstlisting}
536	
537	The third mandatory runtime error (domain-specific: formula uses undeclared element)
538	is caught statically by the type checker (see \code{sem2} above) before execution
539	begins, because ANS resolves all formula symbols at compile time (D1 §4.6). If the
540	checker is bypassed and an element symbol is missing at run time (e.g. inside a
541	\code{for}-in loop over a formula), the interpreter raises:
542	\code{runtime error: formula references undeclared element '<symbol>'} and exits
543	\code{1}.
544	
545	% =====================================================================
546	\section{D1 Consistency Checklist}
547	\sectionrule
548	
549	\begin{table}[h]
550	\centering
551	\rowcolors{2}{anscodebg}{white}
552	\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{} L{4.4cm} L{1.5cm} Y @{}}
553	\toprule
554	\rowcolor{ansblue}
555	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{D1 property}} &
556	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Section}} &
557	\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Demonstrated by}} \\
558	\midrule
559	Static (lexical) scoping &
560	§4.6 &
561	Functions always resolve variables in their declaration environment, not the
562	caller's. \code{for}-in scopes are created and destroyed per iteration. \\
563	Call-by-value &
564	§4.6 &
565	Compound arguments are copied into each function call frame; mutations inside a
566	function do not affect the caller's bindings. \\
567	Strong typing, no implicit coercion &
568	§4.5 &
569	\code{sem4}: argument-count error; \code{sem2}: formula with undeclared element;
570	type mismatches reported rather than silently coerced. \\
571	Name equivalence for domain types &
572	§4.5 &
573	\code{compound water} and a hypothetical second compound with the same formula
574	are distinct; the type checker distinguishes them by name. \\
575	Reaction balance at declaration time &
576	§4.4.2 &
577	\code{sem1}: checker rejects the unbalanced \code{bad\_water} reaction before
578	any statement executes. \\
579	\code{for}-in over Formula &
580	§4.4.1 &
581	\code{molar\_mass} iterates elements in first-appearance order (H then O for
582	H\textsubscript{2}O) producing correct sums. \\
583	\code{for}-in over ReactionSide &
584	§4.4.1 &
585	\code{total\_reactant\_mass} iterates the species of a reaction side; the
586	extended rule added in D1 §4.4 revision. \\
587	\code{for}-in over List &
588	§4.4.1 &
589	\code{classify\_mass} called four times for each compound in the list. \\
590	Division-by-zero runtime error &
591	§4.4 &
592	\code{rt1}: detected at run time with file, line, and message. \\
593	Index-out-of-range runtime error &
594	§4.5 &
595	\code{rt2}: detected at run time with file, line, index, and valid range. \\
596	\bottomrule
597	\end{tabularx}
598	\end{table}
599	
600	% =====================================================================
601	\section{Conclusion}
602	\sectionrule
603	
604	The ANS interpreter and type checker, implemented in C11 in \code{D2/}, pass all
605	required tests. The three valid programs execute end-to-end with correct output,
606	matching the expected values derived from the D1 operational semantics (§4.4).
607	The type checker correctly rejects four classes of semantic error --- unbalanced
608	reactions, undeclared formula elements, missing required fields, and wrong argument
609	counts --- before execution begins. Two runtime errors abort execution cleanly
610	with informative, line-numbered diagnostics.
611	
612	Every property declared in D1 (static scoping, call-by-value, strong typing, no
613	implicit coercion, reaction-balance verification, and both forms of \code{for}-in
614	iteration) is demonstrated in at least one test. The implementation is consistent
615	with the final D1 specification, including the §4.4 extension that adds
616	\code{for}-in over a ReactionSide.
617	
618	\end{document}
