\toprule
\rowcolor{ansblue}
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Evidence item}} & \textcolor{white}{\textbf{D3 P2 coverage}} \\
\midrule
Valid programs (end-to-end) &
  Three programs in \code{D3/programs/} run to completion with correct
  \code{stdout} output. \\
Semantic error detection &
  Four negative type-checker tests in \code{D3/semantic/}; each rejected
  with a precise, line-numbered diagnostic. \\
Runtime error handling &
  Two runtime-error tests in \code{D3/semantic/}; each aborts with an
  informative message and exits \code{1}. \\
D1 consistency &
  Static scoping, call-by-value, strong typing, no implicit coercion, and
  reaction-balance verification are all exercised by the three programs. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

% =====================================================================
\section{End-to-End Execution of Three Valid Programs}
\sectionrule

All three programs are executed with \code{D2/ans --run <file>}.
The type checker runs first; if it passes, the interpreter executes the program.

% ----- Program 1 -----
\subsection{Program 1 --- Molar Mass Classifier (\file{D3/programs/molar\_mass\_demo.ans})}

\textbf{Features exercised:} \code{element} and \code{compound} declarations, typed
function parameter (\code{compound c}), \code{for}-in loop over a Formula
(§4.4.1), member access (\code{c.formula}, \code{element.atomic\_mass}),
formula indexing (\code{formula[element]}), augmented assignment (\code{mass +=}),
\code{if}/\code{elif}/\code{else} with Boolean comparison, list literal and
\code{for}-in over a List.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=ans]
element H { atomic_mass = 1.008,  valence = 1, symbol = "H" };
element O { atomic_mass = 15.999, valence = 2, symbol = "O" };
element C { atomic_mass = 12.011, valence = 4, symbol = "C" };
element N { atomic_mass = 14.007, valence = 3, symbol = "N" };

compound water   { formula = H2O,  name = "Water"          };
compound methane { formula = CH4,  name = "Methane"        };
compound co2     { formula = CO2,  name = "Carbon dioxide" };
compound ammonia { formula = NH3,  name = "Ammonia"        };

function molar_mass(compound c) {
    let formula = c.formula;
    let mass = 0.0;
    for (let element in formula) {
        mass += element.atomic_mass * formula[element];
    }
    return mass;
};

function classify_mass(compound c) {
    let mass = molar_mass(c);
    if (mass < 20.0) {
        print "Light molecule.";
    }
    elif (mass < 50.0) {
        print "Medium molecule.";
    }
    else {
        print "Heavy molecule.";
    }
};

let compounds = [water, methane, co2, ammonia];
for (let c in compounds) {
    classify_mass(c);
};
\end{lstlisting}

\textbf{Trace.} The \code{for}-in over \code{compounds} (a \code{List<Compound>})
calls \code{classify\_mass} four times. Inside each call, \code{molar\_mass}
iterates the compound's formula in left-to-right first-appearance order,
accumulating $\sum (\textit{atomic\_mass} \times \textit{count})$:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\rowcolors{2}{anscodebg}{white}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{} L{2.0cm} L{1.8cm} L{4.8cm} L{1.6cm} L{2.2cm} @{}}
\toprule
\rowcolor{ansblue}
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Compound}} &
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Formula}} &
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Calculation}} &
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Mass (g/mol)}} &
\textcolor{white}{\textbf{Class}} \\
\midrule
\code{water}   & H\textsubscript{2}O   & $2{\times}1.008 + 1{\times}15.999$ & 18.015 & Light ($<20$) \\
\code{methane} & CH\textsubscript{4}   & $1{\times}12.011 + 4{\times}1.008$ & 16.043 & Light ($<20$) \\
\code{co2}     & CO\textsubscript{2}   & $1{\times}12.011 + 2{\times}15.999$ & 44.009 & Medium ($<50$) \\
\code{ammonia} & NH\textsubscript{3}   & $1{\times}14.007 + 3{\times}1.008$ & 17.031 & Light ($<20$) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

